# Object level and meta level

The **object level** of a system is what the system talks about or operates
on. The **meta level** is talk about, or operation on, the system itself: its
symbols, its rules, its proofs. The distinction is what a [[strange-loop]]
needs in order to exist and then violates. Without two levels there is nothing
to cross, and the loop is only a cycle.

## Description

The distinction is relative. Arithmetic has numbers as its object level, and
statements such as "this formula is provable" as its meta level. For a
document system the object level is the content of the cards, and the meta
level is the rules by which cards are read and run.

The discussion uses the distinction in three places, and each time it does the
deciding work.

**Where the levels collapse.** Gödel's construction is the reference case. It
proceeds in the order shown.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
 A[Arithmetic talks about numbers] --> B[Numbers encode arithmetic formulas]
 B --> C[Arithmetic represents proof relations]
 C --> D[Sentence expresses property of its own code]
 D -.self-reference.-> A
```

After the second step, a statement about formulas is also a statement about
numbers. The meta level has been folded into the object level by
[[godel-numbering]]. The discussion names the result the "unexpected collapse
of object-level and metalevel separation" and makes it the test for a strange
loop in place of dynamism or repetition.

**Where the levels stay apart.** The same discussion insists on a distinction
that is easy to lose when building such a construction in software:
*executing the construction externally* is different from *proving that the
theory represents the construction internally*. A program that computes Gödel
numbers stands outside the theory, at the meta level, the whole time. The loop
closes only when the theory itself can express the relation. An experiment in
HQL that runs an encoder has not yet shown that anything refers to itself.

**Where the meta level itself moves.** When a person or agent extends a theory
with a new axiom, the meta level acts on the object level from outside. When
the criteria for choosing axioms are themselves revised, there is a level
above that one. The discussion writes this as a pair, theory and revision
rule, changing together, and separates *static reflection*, in which a theory
expresses properties of its own syntax, from *evolving reflection*, in which
those results change the rules.

There is also a formal limit on the collapse. A theory can represent its own
provability, and it cannot define its own truth; that is the content of
[[tarski-undefinability]]. The levels can be folded together only so far.

## Relations

- It is the precondition of a [[level-crossing]] {relation: part-of}: a crossing is a passage between these two levels.
- A [[strange-loop]] {relation: uses} is what results when the two levels collapse into each other and return.
- In arithmetic the collapse is achieved by [[godel-numbering]] {relation: uses}, which makes statements about formulas into statements about numbers.
- A [[reflective-formal-system]] {relation: example-of} is a theory that carries part of its own meta level inside itself.
- A [[metacircular-interpreter]] {relation: example-of} holds both levels in one language: the interpreter and the program interpreted.
- A [[reflection-progression]] {relation: example-of} moves the boundary step by step, each theory serving as object for the next.
- A [[feedback-loop]] {relation: contrasts} has no meta level: every signal in it is of the same kind.
- It bears on the question [[strange-loop-meaning]] {relation: answers}: the collapse of this separation is the criterion.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[level-crossing]].** The passage of something from one of these levels to
  the other. The present card describes the two sides; that card describes the
  act of going between them.
- **[[godel-numbering]].** An effective coding of formulas and proofs as
  numbers. It is the device that puts the meta level of arithmetic inside its
  object level.
- **[[reflective-formal-system]].** A proposed name for a theory together with
  an encoding of its syntax and internal representations of its proof
  relations. It is the object-and-meta distinction built into one structure.
- **[[reflection-progression]].** A chain of theories, each extending the
  previous one by a statement the previous one could not decide. Each stage
  looks down on the one before as its object.

